+# FAST-SOLVER audit: msolve on the full Moh \(\le 100\) residual order charts
+
+Lane `moh14-msolve-sol56-20260905`.  Date: 2026-09-05 UTC.  Chart root:
+`box/moh14-charts-20260905/`.  Frozen inputs:
+`/tmp/jc2-lane.bWMIVr/inputs/`.
+
+This report is deliberately strict about three different statements:
+
+1. a modular Gröbner basis is the unit ideal in a declared prime field;
+2. the corresponding localized ideal is the unit ideal over
+   \(\mathbb Q\);
+3. a non-unit chart point lifts to a Keller pair.
+
+Only (2), with the correct full chart and the Rabinowitsch localization,
+kills a row by theorem (T).  Only (3) could support a counterexample claim.
+
+---
+
+## 0. Headline and final verdict
+
+`FINAL RESULTS INSERTED AT END OF RUN.`
+
+---
+
+## 1. Frozen-input gate
+
+Before reading or running a charged input, I generated a check file from the
+lane receipt `xmodel/moh14-msolve-sol56-20260905.run.v2`.  The AWK program
+collected the indexed `charged_input_<i>_sha256` and
+`charged_input_<i>_basename` fields into maps and emitted each pair only after
+both maps had been populated.  Thus no digest was copied from the prose task,
+and the check did not depend on receipt-line adjacency.  The generated file
+was `/tmp/moh14-msolve-sol56-20260905.sha256`; `sha256sum -c` returned:
+
+```text
+moh14-fullorder-grok46-20260905.md: OK
+moh14-fix-solve-opus5-20260905.md: OK
+guided_gb.py: OK
+moh_skeleton_full.py: OK
+FALLACY-v2.md: OK
+modular_solve.py: OK
+jacobian_check.py: OK
+sprime3_compiler.py: OK
+```
+
+The gate is therefore **8/8 OK**.  There was no content mismatch.  All
+citations to a charged input below refer to its frozen copy, not to a mutable
+workspace lookalike.
+
+---
+
+## 2. The object that has to be solved
+
+The preceding full-order audit identified the correct necessary object as the
+full Theorem-1.2 descended monomial--Jacobian D1 order chart, not the earlier
+total-degree envelope.  Seven of the twelve old fibres were strict sub-slices;
+the completed full inventories contain 77--378 coefficient unknowns.  See
+`/tmp/jc2-lane.bWMIVr/inputs/moh14-fullorder-grok46-20260905.md:128-160`.
+
+There are twelve fibre charts grouped into six residual classes:
+
+| class | required full fibre(s) | source unknowns before localization |
+|---|---|---:|
+| `C_n16m12_M6_13_ell3_s3` | `V1_1`, `V1_3`, `V4_3` | 333, 378, 155 |
+| `C_n18m12_M2_9_ell2_s3` | `V1_8`, `V3_8` | 120, 88 |
+| `C_n24m16_M12_17_ell1_s3` | `V2_1`, `V1_2`, `V3_2` | 153, 251, 118 |
+| `C_n24m16_Mm12_m2_5_ell1_s4` | `V1_1_6` | 77 |
+| `C_n24m18_M9_20_ell1_s3` | `V1_9`, `V4_9` | 341, 248 |
+| `C_n24m18_Mm15_14_ell1_s3` | `V1_9` | 126 |
+
+The class logic is conjunctive.  A class is dead only when **every** fibre in
+its cover is proved empty.  In particular, killing `M6_13 V4_3` alone would
+not kill the three-fibre `M6_13` class, and killing `M2_9 V3_8` alone would not
+kill the two-fibre `M2_9` class.  The singleton `Mm12` and `Mm15` classes need
+one fibre apiece.
+
+The prior Singular baseline had no unit, no certified nonempty chart, and six
+class timeouts.  Its four already-extracted full fibres were:
+
+| fibre | rows | unknowns | previous full-chart endpoint |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| `Mm15 V1_9` | 176 | 126 | local 1,500 s and r7i 3,600 s timeout |
+| `Mm12 V1_1_6` | 425 | 77 | c7i 1,500 s and r7i 3,600 s timeout; 11.2 GB near 58 min |
+| `M2_9 V3_8` | 159 | 88 | several 900--3,600 s timeouts; 159 generators interreduced to 129, then timeout |
+| `M6_13 V4_3` | 243 | 155 | local 1,500 s timeout |
+
+Those baselines are recorded at
+`/tmp/jc2-lane.bWMIVr/inputs/moh14-fullorder-grok46-20260905.md:262-283`.
+They are the comparison point for this lane; bounded-degree `NO_UNIT` results
+are not survival statements.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Fleet audit and stale-premise correction
+
+All five requested workers were reachable with `~/.ssh/jc2-fleet`:
+
+| workers | CPU | physical RAM | `/usr/bin/msolve` |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| c7i `172.30.0.7`, `.18`, `.28` | 32 vCPU each | about 61 GiB each | Debian/Ubuntu 0.6.5 |
+| r7i `172.30.0.166`, `.254` | 32 vCPU each | about 247 GiB each | Debian/Ubuntu 0.6.5 |
+
+The five distro binaries were byte-identical, SHA-256
+`c2722288d22c3a1eab00c0b818d0204d4a98104d059b2fd4ecb7343e8ef759fa`.
+The prompt's package premise was stale on four hosts: only `.7` imported
+`qqideal 0.2.0`, `msolveio 0.2.1`, and `flint 0.9.0`; `.18`, `.28`, `.166`,
+and `.254` had the msolve executable but not those Python packages.  Emission
+therefore happened on `.7`, and byte-identical `.ms` inputs plus manifests
+were copied to the compute hosts.
+
+The fixed builders themselves were current on every host.  Their combined
+digest was
+`8b42412cd6a5f4e9c3e4a9fc893012a2038a00759c67f0837fe0f27e259dbb96`,
+and `builder_fix.py` was
+`d6662abfec114a443712600f87e3e7b7064071f445d9b177d2f0ea13c9d3836b`.
+However, most generated rows were not current.  The widespread 240-unknown
+`M6_13` and 119-unknown `M2_9` union files were the old envelope systems, not
+the required 333/378- and 120-unknown full fibres.  They were excluded.
+At launch, the only reusable full fibre TSVs were `M6_13 V4_3` and
+`M2_9 V3_8`; the singleton union/fibre files for `Mm12` and `Mm15` were also
+valid because those classes have one fibre.  `M12_17` and `M9_20` had only
+headers.  This is why extraction remained on the critical path despite the
+task saying the generators were already on the workers.
+
+An official upstream 0.10.1 Intel/AVX512 static binary was also installed in
+an isolated chart-box tool directory and tested against the distro binary.
+Its provenance, archive, source tarball, hashes, and controls are preserved
+under
+`box/moh14-charts-20260905/tools/msolve-0.10.1-official-intel-avx512/`.
+On a matched ten-minute `M2_9 V3_8` 32-row-prefix run at \(p=32003\), both
+versions followed the same reduction trajectory and neither returned a
+basis.  The version change alone did not break the wall.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Exact ring, map, and localization custody
+
+### 4.1 Source ideal
+
+For a fibre with metadata variables
+\((u_1,\ldots,u_N)\), source row polynomials
+\(f_1,\ldots,f_r\in\mathbb Z[u_1,\ldots,u_N]\), and localization factor
+`c`, the emitted modular ideal was
+
+\[
+ I_p=(\bar f_1,\ldots,\bar f_r, zc-1)
+ \subset \mathbb F_p[v_1,\ldots,c,\ldots,v_{N-1},z].
+\]
+
+The rational confirmation input, if triggered, is the identical construction
+in \(\mathbb Q[v_1,\ldots,c,\ldots,v_{N-1},z]\).  The variable `c` is never
+added as a generator.  The equation `z*c-1` is the Rabinowitsch encoding of
+the open set \(c\ne0\); omitting it, or adding `c`, would answer a different
+question.
+
+### 4.2 Name map and checks
+
+`msolve_chart.py` reads the pipe-delimited `rows.tsv` and its metadata.  It
+requires the exact header
+
+```text
+source_index|h_power|x_power|y_power|expr
+```
+
+and rejects a header-only file, a repeated header, a blank/malformed row, an
+explicit zero generator, an identifier absent from the metadata ring, a
+duplicate variable, or inconsistent saturation metadata.  Original names
+containing underscores are mapped bijectively to msolve-safe names
+`v1`, `v2`, ...; literal `c` is preserved; `z` is fresh and appended last.
+Every translated polynomial is round-tripped through the inverse token map.
+The manifest records, in order:
+
+- source TSV, metadata, class JSON, and builder paths, sizes, and SHA-256s;
+- original and safe variable order and every individual map pair;
+- source-row and emitted-generator counts;
+- field characteristic and emitter/package versions;
+- literal localization polynomial `c`, slack `z`, and appended `z*c-1`;
+- exact emitted bytes and SHA-256.
+
+The production emitter is `msolveio.emit_system`; there is no hand-written
+format fallback.  Adding `z` changes the solver variable counts from
+77--378 to 78--379.  Adding `z*c-1` changes row counts from \(r\) to
+\(r+1\).
+
+The helper also supports manifest-recorded prefix or explicit-ordinal
+subsets.  These were used only as one-way searches for a cheaper certificate:
+if a selected subset plus `z*c-1` is the unit ideal over \(\mathbb Q\), then
+the full ideal is unit.  A non-unit or timed-out subset says nothing about the
+full ideal and is never labelled a surviving chart.
+
+### 4.3 Prime and linear-algebra modes
+
+The initial screening prime was \(32003\).  A runtime audit found that msolve
+0.6.5 and 0.10.1 both print
+
+```text
+Warning: characteristic is too low for choosing probabilistic linear algebra;
+linear algebra option set to 2
+```
+
+when invoked with `-l 44` at that prime.  Thus the early files labelled `l44`
+actually ran exact sparse linear algebra (`l2`).  This was not hidden in the
+reporting.
+
+I then used `flint.fmpz(1073741827).is_prime()`, which returned true, and
+re-emitted the polynomials through `msolveio` at
+\(p=1073741827\).  I did not edit the characteristic header of a small-prime
+file: rational/integer coefficients were reduced afresh by the emitter.  The
+msolve input banner confirmed `field characteristic 1073741827` and
+`linear algebra option 44`.  Matched traces showed that the important memory
+control was the `-m 1000` pair cap; activating `l44` did not by itself provide
+a demonstrated per-round speedup.  Both facts matter when deciding whether
+this wall is likely to fall to another msolve invocation.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Positive and negative controls
+
+The positive control is the banked D=108, \(\delta=3\), stage-0 kill from
+`box/sysguidedgb-20260903/runs/T2_g108_unit/T2_g108_delta3_stage0_common_h3_Q.sing`.
+Its variables are `jet1,jet2,c,z`, and its six generators include `z*c-1`.
+The contradiction is exact over \(\mathbb Q\), independently of software:
+one generator gives `-2*jet2=0`, another then gives `2*jet1^2=0`, and a third
+reduces to `-c=0`; this contradicts `z*c-1=0`.
+
+The negative control is a tame triangular family in variables
+`p,q,x,y,c,z`:
+
+```text
+p-x-y^2,
+q-y,
+c-1,
+z*c-1
+```
+
+It has the free parameters `x,y`, hence dimension two, and must remain
+non-unit.
+
+| field/run | positive D=108 control | tame-family control |
+|---|---|---|
+| \(\mathbb F_{32003}\), distro 0.6.5 | `UNIT`, basis `[1]` | `NONUNIT`, dimension 2 |
+| \(\mathbb F_{1073741827}\), distro 0.6.5, actual `l44` | `UNIT`, basis `[1]` | `NONUNIT`, dimension 2 |
+| \(\mathbb Q\), distro 0.6.5 | `[1]` signal; exact contradiction above confirms it | `NONUNIT`, dimension 2; three-prime reconstruction |
+
+At the 30-bit prime the positive-control output SHA-256 is
+`1c494fa1141e9ad784a12937e5342ba5f977d656693b6c17717fa3c50dc00bbd`;
+the tame output is
+`819d3c72288786e99d04c84b0e83854a0cc36d273821cbe9c04970c4fdce3b84`.
+The same-field controls therefore catch both accidental loss of the
+localization and the degenerate "everything is unit" failure mode.
+
+---
+
+## 6. Extraction and solve results
+
+`FINAL TABLES INSERTED AT END OF RUN.`
