+# FAST-SOLVER audit: msolve on the full Moh \(\le 100\) residual order charts
+
+Lane `moh14-msolve-sol56-20260905`.  Date: 2026-09-05 UTC.  Chart root:
+`box/moh14-charts-20260905/`.  Frozen inputs:
+`/tmp/jc2-lane.bWMIVr/inputs/`.
+
+This report is deliberately strict about three different statements:
+
+1. a modular Gröbner basis is the unit ideal in a declared prime field;
+2. the corresponding localized ideal is the unit ideal over
+   \(\mathbb Q\);
+3. a non-unit chart point lifts to a Keller pair.
+
+Only (2), with the correct full chart and the Rabinowitsch localization,
+kills a row by theorem (T).  Only (3) could support a counterexample claim.
+
+---
+
+## 0. Headline and final verdict
+
+**PARTIAL: 0 of 6 residual classes are proved UNIT.  The full Moh
+\(\le100\) theorem is not resolved by this lane.**
+
+The controls behaved correctly, five of twelve exact full fibres became
+available, and msolve was run on every available full fibre.  No production
+run returned a Gröbner basis: there is no `[1]`, no positive-dimensional
+basis, and no solution point.  Accordingly none of the twelve residual rows
+is killed here and none is certified to survive.  Seven missing exact fibres
+were also attacked by an end-to-end validated native finite-field builder;
+none finished before the 12:32 UTC stop.
+
+msolve did improve the computational telemetry.  Capping each F4 selection at
+1,000 pairs reduced the worst memory footprint and let `Mm12` encounter degree
+8 rather than dying at the earlier giant degree-7 matrix.  That is useful
+operational progress, but it is not a mathematical verdict and it did not
+materially beat the prior lane in the only sense that closes a class: both
+lanes obtained zero unit ideals.  The current evidence says that the
+\(\le100\) closure still needs either substantially stronger elimination
+engineering or a structural argument.
